    |                                          |
    |                                          |
    |                                          |
    |    %Users-Guide                          |
    |                                          |
    |                                          |
     ------------------------------------------
;BKUG1
%Warning: the sample programs distributed as part of the COMPUZZ System
are for demonstration purposes only. They are not designed for use in the
virtual world. COMPUZZ Computer Systems cannot be held responsible for
any disasters that befall. All wrongs are preserved.
%Contents
    Introduction . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 2
    The COMPUZZ Computer . . . . . . . . . . . 3
    Commands . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 4
    Input Handling . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 4
    Overview of SIMPL/E  . . . . . . . . . . . 6
    SIMPL/E statements . . . . . . . . . . . . 9
    Sensors and Devices  . . . . . . . . . . .11
    Multi-tasking and multi-programming  . . .14
    The CED Editor . . . . . . . . . . . . . .15
    Hints and Tips  .  . . . . . . . . . . . .16
    Some restrictions  . . . . . . . . . . .  20
;BKUG2
 
%Introduction
 
This manual outlines the main facilities of the COMPUZZ family of virtual
machines (in this context, virtual means imaginary). Further
details are to be found in the following manuals:
 
     COMPUZZ System Guide
     COMPUZZ Editor (CED) User's Guide
     SIMPL/E Programming Manual
     COMPUZZ Robot Programming Guide
     COMPUZZ Matter Transmitter Operator's Guide
 
These manuals are probably lying about somewhere in your installation.
;BKUG3
 
%The-COMPUZZ-Computer
The Compuzz computer is a sophisticated virtual machine that offers the
following facilities:
 
     simple command language (extendable through user programs)
     simple, easy to use programming language (SIMPL/E)
     multi-programming and multi-tasking (re-entrant programs)
     inter-process communication and synchronisation
     full-screen editor (CED)
     support for multiple terminals
     sensing and control of the virtual world
     multiple disk-drive support
 
This manual describes COMPUZZ Model 1.%Significant enhancements are
planned for Model 2.
;BKUG4
%Commands
The COMPUZZ system accepts the following commands:
 
   Directory              lists the name and size of the programs on
or Catalog                the current disk
or List
   Active                 lists the name and other statistics about
                          each program running in the system
   ERAse pg               erases a program from the disk
   CANcel pg <nn>         cancels a program which is running
   Edit pg                edits a program
   Clear                  clears the VDU screen
   Type   pg              lists a program on the screen
   DISK <diskname>        lists on-line disks, or switches current
   TELL <(pg <vn>)> text  passes text to a program
 
;BKUG5
%Input-Handling
 
When something is typed at the keyboard of the computer or a terminal,
the COMPUZZ monitor deals with it as follows:
 
  if it is the name of a program, that program is run
 
  else if it is a valid command (not all commands are valid at%terminals)
    that command is obeyed
 
  else if programs are waiting for input, it is passed to the first such
    program (ie an implicit TELL)
 
  else it is treated as an unrecognized input
 
 
;BKUG6
%Overview-of-SIMPL/E
 
%SIMPL/E-Variables
 
 local variables, defined by the VARS statement. If you give one the same
        name as a global, you won't be able to refer to the global
 
 global variables, available for any purpose (GV0-GV9)
 
 global sensors, which can be read but not changed. These pseudovariables
        reflect the virtual world (eg TIMENOW, AIR_QUALITY)
 
 global devices, setting which affects the virtual world (eg LIFT_DOOR)
;BKUG7
%Expressions
There are two types of expression in SIMPL/E:
 
 t_exp  translated expressions   - variables are replaced by their values
 e_exp  evaluated expressions    - operators are applied as well
 
Most SIMPL/E statements use the first type (eg GO can be followed by
a t_exp, giving the power of a computed go to), except:
 
  PULL and VARS  - must be simple list of vars, no translation is done
  IF, SET and WAIT   - evaluation as well as translation is done.
 
%Variable-Substitution
Variables are substituted by their values if they are in%UPPER-CASE.
Thus the statement "say timenow is TIMENOW" will display a message
such as "timenow is 24".
;BKUG8
%Operators
Only one operator is allowed in a SIMPL/E expression, and it must be the
second token. A full description of how the operators work is given in
the System Guide. The operators are:
 
  + - * / > < = \= >= <= || and .
 
Only the '.' is unusual. It works as follows:
 
  in the expression "X . Y"
  if X is not an integer, no evaluation is performed (just translation)
  if X is > 0, the Xth word of the string Y is returned
  if X = 0, the number of words in the string Y is returned
  if X < 0, the Xth word of the string Y is deleted
 
This gives SIMPL/E some basic list processing functions.
;BKUG9
%SIMPL/E Statements
 
 *                                Comment lines (rest ignored)
 VARS list-of-local-variables     Defines, initialises to 0
 GO label                         Transfer control
 IF (expression) statement        Execute statement if expression = 1
 WAIT, WAIT FOR expression        Delay expression ticks
 WAIT UNTIL expression            Delay until expression = 1
 SAY text                         displays text on all enabled screens
 PULL list-of-local-variables     sets vars to input from terminal or
                                  other program (see TELL)
 RUN program                      start program running (asynchronously)
 CALL routine                     execute system command (eg EDIT)
 TELL (program) text              send text to program (see PULL)
 END                              end normally
                                                             ... more
;BKUG10
 
 ABEND text                       abend, displaying text
<SET> var = expression            set local or global variable
 INTERPRET line                   translate and execute line
 
%Labels
SIMPL/E labels consist of a string of alphanumerics (must not be entirely
numeric) separated from the rest of the line by a colon (':'). There must
be at least one blank on either side of the colon.
;BKUG11
%Sensors or global read-only pseudo-variables, are:
  RANDOM      - retrieves a random number between 0 and 999
  TIMENOW     - the current time (in ticks)
  VERSION     - version of the current program
  AIR_QUALITY - of the air you are breathing (1=good,10=bad)
  ENERGY      - available for air_pump, shield etc
  ROBOT_EAR   - set to what you SAY'd, or "hash" if nothing
  ROBOT_EYE   - list of "things" the robot can see (first 4 chars only)
  HUMAN_ACT   - what you did (if in room with robot - "hash" if nothing)
  ALIEN_ACT   - what alien did (")
  ALERT_LEVEL - alien attack level (0=OK, 1=near, 2=attacking, 3=inside)
The following are updated when PROBE is set to ON (see devices)
  HUMAN_HERE  - 1 if you are in the room with the robot, 0 if not
  ALIEN_HERE  - 1 if alien is in the room with the robot
  AIR_HERE    - 1 if there is air in the room with the robot
  POSITION    - set to ROBOT coordinates
;BKUG12
%Devices - global variables that affect the world
  AIR_PUMP       ON or OFF (if off, air in room gets worse
  INTRUDER_ALARM ON or OFF (if on, robot and display warn of intruders
  TERMINALn      ENABLED or DISABLED (if DISABLED, terminal unusable)
  TRACE          ON or OFF (if ON, statement execution is traced)
  SHIELD         ON or OFF (if ON, intruders are repelled)
  LIFT_BUTTON    ON or OFF (no side-effects at all)
  LIFT_DOOR      OPEN or CLOSE (moves lift door)
  LIFT_FLOOR     1, 2 etc (moves lift to floor)
  ROBOT          ROBOT will try to execute the SIMPL/E statement it is
                 set to
  ROBOT_VOICE    ROBOT will speak whatever it is set to
  PROBE          sets HUMAN_HERE, ALIEN_HERE, AIR_HERE and POSITION
                 when set to ON (and is then turned off again)
  MT_MODE        OFF, MANUAL or AUTO (matter transmitter status)
  MT_BEAM        from to (immediate matter transference - AUTO mode only)
Certain devices take time to operate (eg LIFT_FLOOR)
;BKUG14
%MultiTasking-and-MultiProgramming
 
The COMPUZZ monitor allows several programs to be running at the same
time. The limit on the number is set by the size of%"engine" installed
(the typical engine is a CIPPER ie 100 instructions per tick).
 
Several versions of the%same program may run at the same time, each of
which has its own local variables. These different processes using the
same program text are called%versions. It is possible to cancel or send
data to a particular version by following the name with the version
number. For example:
   TELL (MYPROG 3) LIFT_DOOR = OPEN
will pass the data "LIFT_DOOR = OPEN" to version 3 of MYPROG.
;BKUG16
%Hints-and-Tips
 
SIMPL/E programs are inherently re-entrant. In some cases, this can be
undesirable. For example, if there is only one computer-controlled lift
it makes no sense to have two versions of the LIFT program running.
This can be avoided using the VERSION global pseudo-variable. This is
set to the version number of the program, so that if two copies are
already running, starting another will give it a version number of 3.
Thus, to prevent multi-tasking, start your program with the statement:
     IF (VERSION > 1) ABEND to prevent multitasking
 
Note that a separate copy of VERSION exists for each process in the
system (just like local variables). It is a global purely in the sense
that it is not defined on a VARS statement.
;BKUG20
 
%Restrictions.
 
A full list is included as an appendix in the COMPUZZ System Guide.
Please read this carefully. Since COMPUZZ computers are typically used
to control powerful and potentially dangerous devices such as lifts,
burglar alarms, lasers etc, failure to observe the recommended procedures
can be fatal.
;BKPM0
.sssc
