    |                                          |
    |                                          |
    |            ZENO V1.1                     |
    |A delicate%scientific-instrument not a toy|
    |                                          |
    |                                          |
    |    %David-Mitchell ZENO at WINVMB        |
    |     (now zenoshrdlu at gmail.com)        |
    |                                          |
     ------------------------------------------
;BKHE1
 
  Reasons for ZENO's existence  . . . . . . . 2
  The ZENO files  . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 3
  General Introduction to ZENO  . . . . . . . 4
    Comparisons with other games  . . . . . . 4
    Object of the game  . . . . . . . . . . . 4
    The extent of your powers . . . . . . . . 4
  "Objects" . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 5
  Commands  . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 6
  Books and other readable objects  . . . . . 7
  Sample Script for Beginners . . . . . . . . 8
;BKHE2
ZENO is an experimental REX exec (currently in version 1, the initial
prototype). It was written:
 (i)   to demonstrate the expressive power of REX (though the prototype
       should NOT be taken as illustrating good programming style)
 (ii)  to show that an interpreted language CAN provide adequate
       performance, even for a large complex application
 (iii) to prototype a number of "Personal Computer" characteristics
       (e.g. a VDU scroller, command language, full-screen editor etc)
 (iv)  to provide an interactive environment to stimulate the learning of
       problem-solving skills (debugging etc)
 (v)   to experiment with the computer control of a variety of complex
       devices (lifts, robots etc)
 (vi)  for entertainment (outside business hours)
 
Although ZENO takes the appearance of a "computerised fantasy game", it
does have serious educational intent.
;BKHE3
The ZENO package consists of 4 files:
 
  1. ZENO DOC     - similar to this HELP
  2. ZENO EXEC     - a large REX EXEC
  3. ZENO INITDATA - initial data read by the EXEC (using EXECIO)
  4. ZENO IOS3270  - screens used by the EXEC (Help etc)
 
ZENO requires REX Version 3, IOS3270 (a fairly recent one which includes
the .j command) and EXECIO. If EXECIO is not available, STACKIO can be
used instead (just one line at the start of the EXEC).
The EXEC will run in a 512K machine.
 
The current version is highly experimental, and is seriously deficient in
a number of areas (poor descriptions, limited world to explore, nasty
"programming" language etc).
;BKHE4
ZENO takes the form of a "computerized fantasy game" which attempts to
model a "universe" - very limited in scope but within that scope fairly
complex. Like Adventure or Zork, the puzzles it presents are tests of
intellectual skill, insight and intuition rather than physical dexterity.
ZENO differs from such games in four main ways:
 
  1. there is no%magic - no wands, words or wizards.
 
  2. there are no%treasures - your only aim is to survive as long as
     possible
 
  3. you have%very-limited physical powers, since there are%no-verbs
     only%objects
 
  4. you have, through the computer within the game, a%significant amount
     of%control over the progress of the game.
;BKHE5
 
%Objects
You get close to things by mentioning their name, not by saying things
like "take rod" or "kill dragon with sword". Since the parser ignores
words it does not know, "take the rod" and "drop the rod" would both be
treated as "rod". What happens when you mention an object is usually
common sense. For example, "door" will take you through the door if it
is open, or produce a message if not.
 
Complexity in ZENO is reserved for the computer system it embodies.
Your survival is totally dependent on your ability to%program your way
out of trouble.
;BKHE6
%Commands
Certain commands are available in all "rooms" (though not within special
environments such as books, computers, etc):
 
  SAY text    "speaks" text (for talking to robot mainly)
  SUBSET      gets you into CMS Subset
  QUIT        this will take you to the exit screen, which gives you a
              rating, and a chance to re-enter the game.
  SAVE    xxx this will save programs and notes in a file called
              "xxx ZENODATA". If no filename is given,  "ZENO" is used.
 
  RESTORE xxx this will restore programs and notes from a file called
              "xxx ZENODATA". If no filename is given,  "ZENO" is used.
 
In ZENO 1.1, SAVE/RESTORE do%not save the%state of the%game.
;BKHE7
%Books-and-other-readable-objects
 
There are a number of objects that are readable. These include several
books, leaflets, papers, and a notebook which is rather special, since
you can%write in it too.
 
;BKHE8
%Sample-Script-for-Beginners
  go to lift        (just "lift" or "door" will do)
  press the button  ("butt" is enough - just first 4 characters)
                    (nothing happens since no lift program is working)
  read the notebook (again, "note" is enough)
                    (try writing in the notebook)
  go back to room   (you will find you are now carring the book)
  go to computer    (different commands allowed here)
  active            (notice nothing is running)
  list              (notice the scrolling)
  e lift            (you are now in the full-screen editor)
  help              (to find out about the editor)
  ....              (play with editor commands)
  quit              (best not to muck up the lift program yet)
                                                              .... More
;BKHE9
%Sample-Script-for-Beginners (Continued)
  air               (starts the air pump - stops you suffocating!)
  lift              (runs the lift program)
  terminal          (runs the terminal program - asks for input)
  active            (both lift and terminal should be active)
                    (stats indicate next line to execute and when)
  ENABLED           (data for terminal prg - note it didn't take "active"
  PF3               (leave computer)
  lift              (back to lift)
  button            (now something happens)
  ....              (takes a move to get to next floor)
  room              (different place)
  terminal          (not all commands are valid at terminals!!)
 
After that you're on your own - there are books lying about to help.
;BKED0
.sssc
     ------------------------------------------
    |                                          |
    |                                          |
    |    %The-COMPUZZ-Editor                   |
    |                                          |
    |    %CED--V2.0                            |
    |                                          |
    |    %Users-Guide                          |
    |                                          |
    |                                          |
     ------------------------------------------
;BKED2
The full-screen editor is fairly conventional except that%no-time-passes
while you are within it. When a PF key, or ENTER is pressed, the things
that happen are:
 
  1. any changes to data on the screen are made to the file
     (if a line is now blank, it is deleted)
  2. the line commands (at right of each line) are obeyed
  3. the commands (separated by "hash") on the command line are obeyed
  4. the PF key function is obeyed
 
%line Commands:
 
   nnA    adds nn blank lines (default nn is 1)
   nnD    deletes nn lines (including this one - default nn is 1)
   nn"    copies this line nn times (default is 1)
   /      makes this the current line
;BKED3
%Editor Commands
 
  Top             set current line = 1
  Bottom          set current line to last line of file
  Locate string   set current line to next line containing "string"
  /string         ditto
  Getfile file <disk>  get "file" from "disk" (default is current disk)
                 and insert after current line
  DELete nn       delete nn lines (including current line)
  FIle            leave editor (file is updated)
  Quit            leave editor (file unchanged)
  Input <text>    if text is present, insert 1 line containing text
                  else, insert a screenful of blank lines
  nn              move current line forward nn
 -nn              move current line backward nn
 :nn              make line nn the current line
